## 8. AI & Adaptive Engine Architecture

### 8.1 Overview

NeuroNest Kids features a two-layer AI architecture:

**Layer 1 — Client-Side Reactive Difficulty** ([useAdaptiveDifficulty.ts](file:///p:/Project%20nuero/nk5/neuro-frontend/neuronest-kids/src/hooks/useAdaptiveDifficulty.ts))
This hook runs entirely in the browser, updating the difficulty level after every single answer. It requires no network call — just pure local state logic — making it instant and robust to connectivity issues.

**Layer 2 — Cloud-Based Cognitive AI** ([useAdaptiveAI.ts](file:///p:/Project%20nuero/nk5/neuro-frontend/neuronest-kids/src/hooks/useAdaptiveAI.ts) + `adaptive-ai-helper` edge function)
This system handles deeper intelligence: generating personalized encouragement messages, creating hints specific to the current question, maintaining the long-term behavior profile, and making cross-session difficulty recommendations.

### 8.2 Client-Side Reactive Difficulty Engine

```
Answer Given
    │
    ▼
recordCorrect() / recordWrong()
    │
    ├── Update: consecutiveWrong, consecutiveCorrect, currentAccuracy
    │
    ▼
calculateShouldShowHard(newState)
    │
    ├── questionsAnswered < 5?  → False (always start easy)
    ├── accuracy ≥ 80%?         → Show hard every 2nd question
    ├── accuracy < 50%?         → Stay easy
    └── accuracy 50-80%?        → Show hard every 3rd question after Q7
    │
    ▼
getDifficultyLevel(consecutiveWrong, consecutiveCorrect, currentDifficulty, baseLevel)
    │
    ├── consecutiveWrong ≥ 3  → Level 1 (Very Easy)
    ├── consecutiveWrong ≥ 2  → Level 2 (Easy)
    ├── currentDifficulty='hard' AND streak ≥ 5 → Level 5 (Expert)
    ├── currentDifficulty='hard'                 → Level 4 (Hard)
    └── else → Level 3 (Normal, using base profile level)
    │
    ▼
generateQuestion(usedLetters, level)
    │
    └── buildOptions(targetLetter, level)
            ├── level ≤ 2: FAR_APART_CLUSTERS (A, G, M, S, Z…)
            ├── level 3:   Random from all 26 letters
            └── level ≥ 4: CONFUSING_GROUPS matching the target
                            e.g., target=B → [B, D, P, Q]
```

### 8.3 Confusing Letter Groups (Hard/Expert Mode)

The AI specifically uses letter groups that children with ASD or dyslexia are known to confuse:

| Group | Letters | Why Confusing |
|---|---|---|
| Mirror/Shape similar | B, D, P, Q | Vertical and horizontal reflections |
| Zig-zag strokes | M, N, W | Similar angular patterns |
| Open curves | U, V, W | Rounded or spiked opening |
| Round open letters | C, G, O | Circular with varying gaps |
| Vertical strokes | I, J, L | Simple vertical lines |
| Squiggles | S, Z | Diagonal mirror images |
| Line families | A, E, F, H | Horizontal and vertical bars |
| Diagonal strokes | K, R, X, Y | Diagonal elements |

### 8.4 Cloud AI Functions (adaptive-ai-helper)

The Supabase Edge Function supports the following action types:

| Action | Trigger | Response |
|---|---|---|
| `get_encouragement` | consecutiveWrong ≥ frustration_threshold | Personalised motivational message |
| `generate_hint` | Player requests help | Question-specific hint string |
| `real_time_support` | Background check every N answers | `{shouldShowHint, shouldSlowPace, shouldTakeBreak, uiAdjustments}` |
| `session_summary` | End of game | Summary of performance with encouragement |
| `adjust_difficulty` | End of session | `{newDifficulty, recommendedPace}` |

### 8.5 Behavior Profile Update Lifecycle

```
Game Session Ends
      │
      ▼
updateStats(correctAnswers, totalQuestions, gameType)
      │
      ├── Compute new running average accuracy
      ├── If accuracy ≥ 80%: add gameType to strong_categories
      ├── If accuracy < 50%: add gameType to challenging_categories
      └── Write updated averages + categories to child_behavior_profiles
      │
      ▼
adjustDifficulty(gameData) [async, calls edge function]
      │
      └── Write new current_difficulty_level to child_behavior_profiles
```

### 8.6 AI Indicator UI Component

The [GameAIIndicator](file:///p:/Project%20nuero/nk5/neuro-frontend/neuronest-kids/src/components/GameAIIndicator.tsx#40-239) component floats in the bottom-right corner of every game. It shows:
- A 5-bar difficulty meter (colored: green→teal→blue→orange→red)
- Current status: Struggling / Focused / Excelling
- Streak count
- Preferred pace from behavior profile
- AI recommendation message (from `useAdaptiveDifficulty.aiRecommendation`)
- Recent AI call history (debugging trace)

